{"connection_id": "BC-a55b77cd0bfae2d4ea56", "ref": "Augustine, Confessions 1.6.7", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "God supplies an infant through the mother's breasts", "note": "Augustine thanks God for providing infant food through the mother's breasts rather than treating maternal nursing as self-sufficient. This Christian reading closely parallels the commentary's distinction between the visible mother and the true divine guardian of a child's nurture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fecfb009ce98bce38fed", "ref": "Ephrem, Hymns on the Nativity 11", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "virgin's womb holds the Provider as an infant", "note": "Ephrem's Nativity hymn dwells on the paradox that the Provider enters the Virgin's womb and accepts infant need. It is a Syriac Christian theological rereading of womb as dwelling and nurture; stanza numbering varies across editions."}
{"connection_id": "BC-82f6ddd6559b2b5f082c", "ref": "Irenaeus, Against Heresies 4.38.1", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "God creates humans to grow toward perfection", "note": "Irenaeus argues that created humanity must grow gradually toward perfection rather than receive its final capacity all at once. This patristic account gives a theological analogue to the commentary's definition of terbiye as movement through states to completion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37b50ae1bced724d9b67", "ref": "Odes of Solomon 19", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "painless virginal delivery in a Christian hymn", "note": "The ode's birth image is commonly read as portraying the Virgin's delivery without pain. Against the commentary's laboring maternal body, it offers a specifically Christian theological reversal rather than a shared account; the ode's transmission and verse division warrant caution."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6bbe4b4a3d1680ee308d", "ref": "Peshitta, Luke 1:78", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Syriac r-ḥ-m mercy vocabulary", "note": "The commentary labels Arabic ر ح م as the root of mercy; the Syriac Peshitta uses the cognate r-ḥ-m word family for God’s mercies in this verse, where mercy brings the dawn to those in darkness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a6ab516c8bfc7dca07c", "ref": "Protevangelium of James 7:2", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Mary's childhood nourishment in the temple", "note": "In the common chapter division, three-year-old Mary is placed at the temple and receives food from an angel. This infancy tradition adds a distinctly Christian scene of a child reared outside her parents' home; chapter and verse divisions vary by translation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f02889aa8b016f338d0", "ref": "Protevangelium of James 8", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Joseph assigned as Mary's guardian", "note": "In the usual chapter division, the priests assign the older Joseph to keep Mary when she can no longer remain in the temple. This infancy tradition extends the commentary's nonbirth-caregiver question into a Christian guardianship narrative; details and chapter subdivision vary by translation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3b02e9c042c12a870f53", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.13.11", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "leaving childhood ways for adulthood", "note": "Paul uses the passage from child to adult as the figure for a completed mode of knowing. It is a brief but exact New Testament staging of maturation that belongs beside terbiye's movement toward completion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d27e94443551a2d8423", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.2", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "teacher feeds learners milk", "note": "The commentary calls a teacher one who feeds people small portions of knowledge; Paul says he fed the Corinthians milk rather than solid food because they were not ready for it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8b5d7dcbb2182e28037c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.2", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "teacher gives milk before solid food", "note": "Paul says he gave the Corinthians milk rather than solid food because they were not ready. It is the clearest New Testament analogue to the commentary's teacher who nourishes people first with small portions of knowledge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e0ced4543cb53a460c16", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.4.15", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "spiritual fatherhood exceeds guardianship", "note": "Paul contrasts many guardians in Christ with his singular fatherhood through the gospel. It gives a particularly close Christian distinction between a supervising caregiver and a formative parent, matching the commentary's careful nonbirth-parent vocabulary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-021abc3801b29fb677ff", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.3.17", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "compassion must become help", "note": "The commentary defines mercy as tenderness that requires beneficence toward its object, especially the weak; John asks how God’s love can remain in someone who closes his inward compassion to a brother in need."}
{"connection_id": "BC-665a2cfafd8331a9097b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.3.17", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "compassion proved by material help", "note": "One who sees a sibling in need and closes the inward organs of compassion is challenged to show God's love. The verse makes compassion concrete through goods and need, matching the commentary's insistence that mercy entails beneficent action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bb655c91ca3e930c3325", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.2", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "newborns grow on milk", "note": "The commentary’s child grows through successive stages under nurture; Peter urges newborn infants to long for pure spiritual milk so they may grow into salvation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-932acd59599ad5f9c271", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.2", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "newborns crave milk in order to grow", "note": "Newborn infants are urged to desire pure spiritual milk so that they may grow toward salvation. The verse makes milk, infancy, teaching, and purposeful maturation one integrated image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d7d63e8bdc7f50f9b73e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.2.11", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a father addresses his children", "note": "The commentary pairs the mother’s womb and a father’s role in raising a child; Paul recalls exhorting and encouraging each Thessalonian as a father addresses his own children."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c41e0bac458dad756426", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.2.11", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "father exhorts, encourages, and comforts children", "note": "Paul also compares his work to a father dealing with each child through exhortation, encouragement, and consolation. Together with maternal nursing in the preceding image, it makes cultivation a patient parental practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d6660e12739b123f95b6", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.2.12", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "parental guidance toward a worthy life", "note": "The commentary says upbringing leads step by step toward completion; Paul says he urged the community to live worthily of God, like a father guiding children toward their calling."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2196bb0cca36ad3ff0c5", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.2.7", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "nursing mother’s tenderness", "note": "The commentary’s rahma is tenderness expressed as care and its Rab image is nurturing; Paul says he was gentle among the Thessalonians like a nursing mother cherishing her own children."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76e04959f1eaa17b8ef0", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.2.7", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "nursing mother cherishes her own children", "note": "Paul depicts his gentle pastoral care as a nursing mother cherishing her children. The image links bodily nurture and nonbiological teaching care in precisely the double register developed by the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e74d660cf9d517bd15a2", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.2.8", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sharing life with those nurtured", "note": "The commentary describes care as more than a feeling, extending to an actual good for the cared-for; Paul says he shared not only the gospel but his own life with the community because they had become dear to him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-84eb6a57a854d1781ba8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.3.5", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "managing one's household and caring for God's assembly", "note": "The argument reasons from managing one's own household to caring for God's church. It is a direct Christian development of the commentary's claim that standing up a household's affairs is a genuine form of care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c07eccd43016936d9a6b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.5.4", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "children repay care at home", "note": "The commentary links mercy toward parents to their raising the child while small; Paul says descendants should learn to show piety at home and make some return to their parents and grandparents."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce04300451d28a2e2d6e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.5.4", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "children repay parents through care for their household", "note": "Children and grandchildren must first practice piety toward their own household and make return to their parents. This is a direct New Testament counterpart to the commentary's Qur'an 17:23–24 reversal from parental raising to a child's merciful responsibility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0709ec3bf07f51f41fe8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.5.8", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "provide for one’s household", "note": "The commentary’s household upholder supplies and protects the family; Paul says providing for one’s own household is a concrete family duty and failure repudiates the faith."}
{"connection_id": "BC-73da5f6d36d4b4dc3331", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.12.14", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "parents store up for children", "note": "Paul invokes the household norm that parents should save for children, not children for parents. It directly connects parenthood with provision, complementing the commentary's domestic food and child-rearing images."}
{"connection_id": "BC-be3318a3e2709b6df951", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.3.15", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sacred writings known from infancy", "note": "Timothy has known the sacred writings from infancy, which can make him wise for salvation. It is a direct image of instruction beginning in childhood and reaching mature discernment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b41ad72eaa485433832a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.20", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses nurtured in his father's house", "note": "Stephen says Moses was brought up for three months in his father's house. The retelling explicitly foregrounds the protected natal home before exposure, which the commentary contrasts with later palace claims."}
{"connection_id": "BC-522f7422113b04987b55", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.21", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses adopted by Pharaoh’s daughter", "note": "The commentary recounts Moses’ exposure and return to his mother; Stephen recalls Pharaoh’s daughter taking and raising him as her own son, a Christian retelling that foregrounds the adoptive household."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87b5988157a06e7a828c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.21", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Pharaoh's daughter raises Moses as son", "note": "Acts says that Pharaoh's daughter took up the exposed Moses and raised him as her own son. It preserves the foster-child transition central to the commentary's distinction between birth, nursing, and a later household's nurture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f8450377c4c0218246c4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.22", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses educated in the palace", "note": "The commentary calls Pharaoh’s “we raised you” only a partial claim; Stephen says Moses was instructed in all Egyptian wisdom and was powerful in words and deeds, preserving palace education alongside the commentary’s emphasis on his mother and God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eef7e562dadde9753d3d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.22", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses instructed in Egyptian wisdom", "note": "Stephen says Moses was instructed in all the wisdom of the Egyptians and became powerful in words and deeds. It supplies the educational continuation of the foster-household narrative, relevant to the commentary's move from being raised to being taught."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b3e68d1285d0c741a7eb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.21", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "parents must not crush children", "note": "The commentary presents raising a child as beneficial formation toward completion; Colossians warns fathers not to embitter children lest they lose heart, marking a limit on parental authority."}
