{"connection_id": "BC-19421e694440b662afd6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.5.29", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "nourishing and cherishing one’s own", "note": "The commentary describes a home that nurtures and protects its members; Ephesians says no one hates his own body but nourishes and cherishes it, then uses that care to explain Christ’s care for the church."}
{"connection_id": "BC-da8eda7c51a2ecf554d8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.5.29", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "nourishing and cherishing as embodied care", "note": "A person nourishes and cherishes his own body, as Christ does the church. The verb for nourishing extends the commentary's rearing imagery into a Christian account of sustained, intimate care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f0c053ecc09ca7cc1d7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.6.1", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "children obey parents", "note": "The commentary’s parent-child frame moves from receiving nurture to responding to parents; Ephesians addresses children directly and commands obedience in the Lord."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5a68f9154aca7d3146f2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.6.2", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "children honor father and mother", "note": "The commentary pairs worship of God with doing good to parents who raised the child; Ephesians repeats the command to honor father and mother, grounding filial duty in the commandment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a9eef913992137fac5c8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.6.4", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "parents raise children toward maturity", "note": "The commentary defines Rab as raising a child toward completion; Ephesians tells fathers to bring children up in the Lord’s discipline and instruction, placing education inside parental care."}
