{"connection_id": "BC-3f177270e43e916ae6a4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.4.2", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "child placed under guardians and household managers", "note": "The heir, while a child, is under guardians and managers until the father's appointed time. This is a particularly exact legal and domestic analogue for the commentary's nonbirth caregiver and the person who maintains a household's affairs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28009238070d77bd2839", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.4.4", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's son born of a woman", "note": "God sends his Son, born of a woman and under the law. The verse keeps incarnation within a concrete maternal birth while the surrounding argument moves toward adoption, furnishing both sides of the commentary's womb and foster-family frame."}
{"connection_id": "BC-145bdb8dfd3eafd45a7e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.4.5", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "adoption into God’s family", "note": "The commentary notes a spouse raising a child who is not biologically theirs; Paul says Christ redeems those under the law so they may receive adoption as children, applying household adoption to believers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9d290c645d2582a0702b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.24", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Moses refuses Pharaoh’s daughter’s sonship", "note": "The commentary calls Pharaoh’s claim to have raised Moses only partial; Hebrews says Moses, when grown, refused to be called the son of Pharaoh’s daughter, making royal upbringing distinct from the identity he chose."}
{"connection_id": "BC-858d78c813798346f270", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.11", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "discipline yields trained righteousness", "note": "Discipline is painful in the present but later yields peaceful fruit of righteousness to those trained by it. Its temporal account of costly formation is a close conceptual partner to terbiye as gradual maturation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1449ff794ab609b052de", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.13", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straight paths heal what is weak", "note": "After the discipline argument, hearers are told to make straight paths for their feet so that what is lame may be healed. It links the commentary's straight path to formative care for the weak rather than to direction alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0b1d49256ef108ddf4c3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.5", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fatherly discipline addressed to a son", "note": "The exhortation cites the address, \"My son,\" and tells the child not to reject the Lord's discipline. This supplies the explicit father-son language whose later training result appears in Heb. 12:11."}
