{"connection_id": "BC-fb72ea00d5dcab334c52", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.16", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "compassion fails without food and clothing", "note": "James rejects telling an inadequately clothed and hungry sibling to depart in peace without giving what the body needs. It makes the commentary's mercy-to-beneficence claim a sharply practical test involving household necessities."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9d9efd94d9ebcec9f6e0", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.1.13", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "birth from God rather than blood or human will", "note": "The prologue describes those born not from blood, fleshly will, or a man's will, but from God. It offers a Johannine redescription of descent that stands in productive tension with the commentary's bodily womb and kinship account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e15fd87307fed90e7b6a", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.1.38", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "source ر ب ب: ῥαββί glossed as Teacher", "note": "John preserves the Semitic address ῥαββί and immediately glosses it as Teacher. As an actual r-b-b cognate, it substantiates the commentary's carefully developed extension from Rabb to a teacher rather than relying on the root label alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-07a19a713e0bf1006973", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.16.21", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "labor pain and joy after birth", "note": "The commentary recalls pain in the womb after delivery; Jesus compares grief followed by joy to a woman distressed in labor who rejoices after her child is born, preserving the passage’s focus on labor and birth rather than postpartum illness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5b94d1d4870688cf4c22", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.16.21", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "birth pain gives way to joy at the child's arrival", "note": "Jesus uses a woman in labor whose anguish turns to joy when a human being is born. It directly meets the commentary's emphasis on the bodily cost of birth, while assigning that image an eschatological meaning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20d02e85f82f95215ce9", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.19.26", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "new family formed at the cross", "note": "The commentary describes kinship through a shared womb and care within a household; Jesus entrusts his mother to the beloved disciple, forming a caregiving family relation not based on common birth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-446c1b0620cff6a6f2da", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.19.27", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the disciple takes Mary into his home", "note": "The commentary describes kinship through a shared womb and care within a household; after entrusting his mother to the disciple, Jesus’ words lead the disciple to take her into his own home, making protection concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-08a9bfafa28b514377df", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.19.27", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a chosen disciple takes Jesus' mother into his home", "note": "Jesus establishes a new mother-son relation, and the disciple takes her into his own household. This is a concrete Christian scene of nonbiological family care that belongs beside the commentary's step-parent and child-minder images."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8267b3e78397a78bf19f", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.20.16", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Aramaic Rabbouni / teacher", "note": "The commentary labels Arabic ر ب ب as the root behind “Rab” and associates it with raising and teaching; Mary addresses Jesus as Rabbouni, “my teacher,” in the scene where she recognizes him after the resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ea16b82bfcba23124c55", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.3.4", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "returning to the mother's womb questioned", "note": "Nicodemus asks whether an adult can enter a mother's womb a second time and be born. The question makes physical womb-life explicit, while the ensuing Johannine discourse redirects birth to a different, Spirit-centered register rather than the commentary's original womb-house."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7780a2710322aa8e95f8", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.6.45", "target": "sec4", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all will be taught by God", "note": "Jesus cites the promise that all will be taught by God. The direct divine-teacher formulation is a New Testament companion to the commentary's Rahman who teaches the Qur'an and teaches human expression."}
